===Verb===
{{en-verb|++}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To regard (someone or something) as [[horrifying]] or [[detestable]]; to feel great [[repugnance]] toward. {{defdate|First attested from around (1350 to 1470).}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=4}}</ref>
#: {{syn|en|detest|disdain|loathe}}
#: {{ux|en|I absolutely '''abhor''' being stuck in traffic jams.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Romans|12|9|passage=Let loue bee without dissimulation: '''abhorre''' that which is euill, cleaue to that which is good.}}
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=April 20, 2003
|author=w:Henry G. Brinton
|title=A Congregation Divided
|journal=w:The Washington Post
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240128195936/https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/opinions/2003/04/20/a-congregation-divided/8e0932a2-efa5-4ea3-b483-fa5333ac4601/
|archivedate=28 January 2024
|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/opinions/2003/04/20/a-congregation-divided/8e0932a2-efa5-4ea3-b483-fa5333ac4601/
|issn=0190-8286
|oclc=2269358
|url2=https://archive.ph/YpIZI
|text=I have risked alienating some members with criticism of the war, reminding them, for example, that the Lord '''abhors''' our worship of the false gods of Western affluence, worldly power and high technology. I agree with {{w|Michael J. Easley}}, the senior pastor-teacher of Immanuel Bible Church in Springfield, when he says, "I think my 'job' is to clearly teach the Scriptures, not be persuaded by what may or may not be our people's views."}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete|impersonal}} To fill with horror or [[disgust]]. {{defdate|Attested from the mid 16<sup>th</sup> century until the early 17<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello|IV|ii|332|2|But neuer taynt my Loue. I cannot say Whore,<br>It do's '''abhorre''' me now I speake the word,<br>To do the Act, that might the addition earne,<br>Not the worlds Masse of vanitie could make me.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To turn aside or avoid; to keep away from; to reject.
# {{lb|en|transitive|canon law|obsolete}} To [[protest]] against; to reject [[solemnly]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 8|II|iv|216|2|I vtterly '''abhorre'''; yea, from my Soule<br>Refuse you for my Iudge, whom yet once more<br>I hold my most malicious Foe, and thinke not<br>At all a Friend to truth.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To feel horror, [[disgust]], or [[dislike]] (towards); to be [[contrary]] or [[averse]] (to); {{non-gloss|construed with ''from''}}. {{defdate|Attested from the mid 16<sup>th</sup> century until the mid 17<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{RQ:Elyot Governour|chapter=That all daunsinge is nat to be reproued|page=86|passage=Also in those daunces were enterlased dities of wanton loue or ribaudry, with frequent remembrance of the moste vile idolis Venus and Bacchus, as it were that the daunce were to their honour and memorie, whiche most of all '''abhorred''' from Christes religion, sauerynge the auncient errour of paganysme.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Divorce|edition=2nd|book=II|chapter=VII|page=46|passage=Either then the law by harmless and needful dispenses, which the gospel is now made to deny, must have anticipated and exceeded the grace of the gospel, or else must be found to have given politic and superficial graces without real pardon, saying in general, “do this and live,” and yet deceiving and damning underhand with unsound and hollow permissions; which is utterly '''abhorring''' from the end of all law, as hath been shewed.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} Differ entirely from. {{defdate|Attested from the mid 16<sup>th</sup> century until the late 17<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>

